aquinas-st-i-ii-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1 and aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.3 have "lower_follow_higher" and "lower_follow_higher → (joy_increased → overflow)"; aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.2 has "joy_from_justice ∧ joy_increased ∧ lower_follow_higher → overflow" and "lower_follow_higher"; they differ when only "the lower powers follow the movement of the higher" and "the joy of justice is increased through the perfection of justice" are true, which the second allows
aquinas-st-i-ii-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1 and aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the lower powers follow the movement of the higher" and "the joy of justice is increased through the perfection of justice" are true and "joy results from the act of justice, at least in the will" and "the joy of justice overflows into the sensitive appetite" are false. Is S4, "And if this joy be increased through the perfection of justice, it will overflow into the sensitive appetite; in so far as the lower powers follow the movement of the higher, as stated above (Q. 17, A. 7; Q. 24, A. 3).", true then?
aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1 has "joy_from_justice → joy_increased" and "joy_increased ∧ overflow → perfect_more_passion"; aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.2 has "joy_increased" and "overflow → perfect_more_passion"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.3 has "overflow → perfect_more_passion"
aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1 and aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if joy results from the act of justice, at least in the will, then the joy of justice is increased through the perfection of justice"; aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.3 does not
aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1 and aquinas-st-i-ii-2.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if joy results from the act of justice, at least in the will, then the joy of justice is increased through the perfection of justice"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
